@startuml FRONTEND_PATTERNS_LAYERED_VIEW
' ==============================================================================
' THE SAME CODEBASE, but organised by PATTERN / RESPONSIBILITY instead of by
' folder. Read this after FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM.puml, which is the
' literal folder map. Each layer only talks to the layer directly below it -
' that discipline is what makes each layer independently testable and
' replaceable.
' ==============================================================================
' KEEPING THIS FILE HONEST
' ==============================================================================
' Last checked against frontend/src: 2026-09-27, after feature 16c.
'
' This diagram names real modules as examples of each pattern, so a rename or a
' new feature area leaves it quietly wrong. Anything that adds a page, a hook, a
' rules module or a feature api.ts should be reflected here in the same branch,
' and so should anything that breaks the "a layer only calls the one below it"
' rule, because that is the claim this diagram actually makes.
' ==============================================================================
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam wrapWidth 280
title FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE - BY LAYER / PATTERN\n(a beginner's map of "why is the code shaped like this")

package "1. ROUTING & GUARD LAYER" as L1 #FFF3CD {
  component "createBrowserRouter\n(routes.tsx)" as R1
  component "Layout route pattern\n(<Outlet/> + nested routes)" as R2
  component "Auth guard component\n(SitzungsWaechter.tsx)" as R3
  note bottom of L1
    **Pattern: declarative routing + layout routes.**
    Instead of "if url == X render Y" scattered around, one data
    structure lists every URL. A route with no path of its own,
    just an <Outlet/>, can wrap its children in shared behaviour
    (a guard, a shell) without every child needing to know about it.

    The guard answers exactly one question - "is anybody signed in" -
    and deliberately no more. **No route checks a role.** The server
    decides who may read what, and a second opinion in the route
    table could only ever be the wrong one. Pages turn the server's
    refusal into words with a way onward instead.
  end note
}

package "2. PAGE / CONTAINER LAYER" as L2 #FFDAB9 {
  component "\"*Seite.tsx\" components\n(ProtokolleSeite, ProtokollSeite,\nPrueflisteSeite, PruefungsSeite,\nBenutzerlisteSeite, KontoAnlegenSeite,\nAnmeldungSeite)" as P1
  component "useProtokollZustand.tsx\n(the shared loading / not-found /\nno-permission cascade)" as P2
  note bottom of L2
    **Pattern: Container (a.k.a. \"smart\") components.**
    A page component's job: read the URL (useParams), fetch data
    (useQuery), handle top-level loading/error states, and hand
    clean data down as props. It should contain very little JSX
    of its own - that's the next layer's job.

    When two pages hit the same dead ends before they reach their
    data, that cascade is lifted into a hook that returns what to
    render INSTEAD (useProtokollZustand), rather than copied. Both
    id-addressed protocol pages did hold their own copy of all four
    states until the branch review on 2026-09-15.
  end note
}

package "3. FEATURE COMPOSITION LAYER" as L3 #FFDAB9 {
  component "ProtokollFormular.tsx\n+ AbschnittN.tsx" as F1
  component "<FormProvider> / <NurLesenContext>" as F2
  note bottom of L3
    **Pattern: composition + React Context.**
    Big screens are built by composing many small components rather
    than one huge file. Cross-cutting data that many descendants need
    (the whole form's state; "are we in read-only mode") is put in a
    Context Provider ONCE near the top, instead of passed down as
    props through every intermediate component ("prop drilling").
  end note
}

package "4. PRESENTATIONAL / REUSABLE UI LAYER" as L4 #D4EDDA {
  component "felder/ (FeldText, FeldAuswahl, ...)\n+ FeldRahmen wrapper" as U1
  note bottom of L4
    **Pattern: presentational (a.k.a. \"dumb\") components +\nshared wrapper (\"compound component\"-ish).**
    These components don't fetch data and mostly don't hold state -
    they take props (a field name, a label key) and render UI, wired
    to the form via a hook (useFormContext). All 338 fields reuse
    ~7 of these, so a label/ARIA/error fix in FeldRahmen.tsx fixes
    every field on the site at once.
  end note
}

package "5. HOOKS / CLIENT LOGIC LAYER" as L5 #D4EDDA {
  component "useAutoSave, useAbsenden,\nuseUebergang, useSitzung,\nuseAnlagen, useEinlesen,\nuseHerunterladen, useKontoAnlegen,\nuseLoeschen, useFeldFehler, useNurLesen" as H1
  note bottom of L5
    **Pattern: custom hooks.**
    React's mechanism for reusing STATEFUL logic across components
    without inheritance or wrapper components. A hook is just a
    function whose name starts with "use" and which may call other
    hooks (useState, useEffect, useQuery...) internally. Each hook
    above owns exactly one concern and is unit-tested on its own
    (see *.test.ts next to each one) using fake timers / a stub
    fetchImpl, with no real browser needed.
  end note
}

package "6. PURE DOMAIN LOGIC LAYER" as L6 #D4EDDA {
  component "regeln/ (schema.ts, prozent.ts,\nhydrologie.ts, vorfluter.ts, arten.ts, ...)" as D1
  component "entwurf/speicherzustand.ts,\nauth/weiter.ts, auth/startseite.ts,\nauth/rollen.ts, api/wiederholen.ts" as D2
  component "absenden/verortung.ts + gruppierung.ts,\npruefliste/parameter.ts + pfad.ts,\npruefung/entscheidungen.ts,\nverwaltung/benutzer/eingabe.ts,\nfelder/pflicht.ts" as D3
  note bottom of L6
    **Pattern: pure functions, framework-free.**
    Deliberately importing nothing from React. A function like
    sichererWeiterPfad(url) or fehlerZustand(error) takes a value,
    returns a value, and is tested with plain input/output
    assertions - no rendering, no mocking a browser. This is the
    layer coding-standards.md means by "logic where a wrong answer
    is possible gets a unit test beside it."

    It is also where the same rule stops being written twice. Each
    file in regeln/ is the client-side half of a rule the backend
    enforces again in Python, and the two are meant to be read and
    changed together. felder/pflicht.ts goes one further and reads
    the required-field list out of the SAME seed file the backend
    reads, because when the two sides kept their own copies they
    drifted invisibly.
  end note
}

package "7. DATA / SERVICE LAYER" as L7 #A8DADC {
  component "api/client.ts\napiAnfrage() <- the only fetch() for JSON\napiDatei()   <- the only fetch() for a file" as A1
  component "one small api.ts per feature:\nentwurf/, anlagen/, pruefung/,\npruefliste/, verwaltung/benutzer/" as A2
  component "TanStack Query\n(useQuery / useMutation / cache,\napi/queryClient.ts, api/wiederholen.ts)" as A3
  note bottom of L7
    **Pattern: Service/Repository layer + server-state cache.**
    Two separate jobs, often confused by beginners:
      - "How do I talk to the network" -> api/client.ts (mechanics:
        cookies, JSON, typed errors). Feature api.ts files are thin
        wrappers naming ONE call each (e.g. speichereAntworten()).
      - "Where do I cache what the server told me, and how do I know
        if it's stale" -> TanStack Query. This is explicitly NOT the
        same thing as component state (useState): server data is
        cached, shared across components via a queryKey, and can be
        invalidated/refetched - useState can't do any of that.

    apiDatei() is separate from apiAnfrage() rather than a flag on it,
    because the two disagree about what a SUCCESS is: one parses the
    body, the other must not touch it. The refusal path is shared, so
    a refused download is the same typed ApiFehler as a refused save
    and the screen already knows how to say it.

    api/wiederholen.ts is the one retry policy every list shares:
    try a dropped request again, never re-send a settled refusal.
    The single documented exception to this layer is useAutoSave,
    which calls apiAnfrage() directly rather than through useMutation
    for a measured performance reason - see the folder map.
  end note
}

package "8. CROSS-CUTTING CONCERNS" as L8 #E8F4FD {
  component "i18n/ (react-i18next,\nresources.d.ts, DatumsProvider)" as C1
  component "theme/ (tokens.ts, muiTheme.ts)\n+ styles/theme.css, hooks/useTheme.ts" as C2
  note bottom of L8
    **Pattern: externalise what changes independently of logic.**
    Strings move to translation files so a second language
    (feature 17, English) needs no component changes. Colours/spacing
    move to CSS custom properties + one MUI theme config so dark mode
    and the "must not look like stock Material" rule are enforced in
    ONE file instead of scattered `sx={{color: '#...'}}` overrides.
  end note
}

cloud "FastAPI backend" as BE #FDF0E3

L1 --> L2
L2 --> L3
L3 --> L4
L3 --> L5
L4 --> L5
L5 --> L6
L5 --> L7
L4 --> L8
L3 --> L8
L7 --> BE

legend bottom
  **Reading order for someone new to this codebase:**
  1. Start at routes.tsx (layer 1) - it is the table of contents for every screen.
  2. Open the "*Seite.tsx" for the page you care about (layer 2) - see what it fetches.
  3. Follow that data into its composition components (layer 3) - see how it's laid out.
  4. Only then look at felder/ (layer 4) if you need to change how ONE kind of
     input behaves - a change there affects every field of that type, everywhere.
  5. Hooks (layer 5) and regeln/ (layer 6) are where the actual interesting
     LOGIC lives, and are the easiest layers to unit-test in isolation - when
     in doubt about "what does this feature actually DO", read here, not the JSX.
  6. api/client.ts (layer 7) is the seam between "frontend" and "backend" -
     see DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml for what happens the instant a request crosses it.
endlegend

@enduml
